"Adversarial Attacks Against Automated Fact-Checking: A Survey (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08463v1)\nciteturn29790view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29789view1\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 1036\nL68: (2019a)】; cite54†Mamta and Cocarascu (2025) ; (2) Adversarial evidence attacks: Injecting manipulated or fabricated evidence into the corpus to mislead the retrieval process or verdict prediction cite55†Du et al.\nL69: (2022) ; cite51†Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) ; and (3) Adversarial claim-evidence pair attacks: Generating synthetic pairs that maintain the original claim-evidence relationship superficially, while embedding contradictory or misleading content that confuses models trained only on truthful data cite56†Schuster et al. (2019) .\nL70: These risks are becoming increasingly salient given recent shifts in content moderation practices.\nL71: For example, Meta has discontinued its partnerships with professional human fact-checkers and transitioned to a community-driven model similar to “Community Notes.”^{3}^{3} 3 https://about.fb.com/news/2025/01/meta-more-speech-fewer-mistakes/ While such changes aim to democratize content moderation, they also place greater reliance on scalable, automated FC systems which must be robust to adversarial manipulation in open environments.\nL72: To build trustworthy AFC systems, resilience against such attacks is essential. Proactively identifying vulnerabilities and designing adversary-aware FC models is key to ensuring their reliability in real-world settings cite51†Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) ; cite54†Mamta and Cocarascu (2025) . Although recent years have seen increasing attention to this area, adversarial attacks against AFC systems remain underexplored.\nL73: While isolated studies have investigated specific attack vectors or datasets, there is a lack of holistic understanding of how diverse adversarial strategies affect the end-to-end AFC pipeline. Several open questions remain:\nL74:   1. 1.\nL75: \nL76: What are the state-of-the-art adversarial techniques targeting the AFC pipeline? (Sec. cite9†4 )\nL77: \nL78:   2. 2.\nL79: \nL80: How well have current defenses developed in response to existing attacks? (Sec. cite19†6 )\nL81: \nL82:   3. 3.\nL83: \nL84: What are future directions for resilient, attack-aware AFC systems? (Sec. cite23†7 )\nL85: Comparison with existing surveys. While most existing surveys on FC focus on system development under benign conditions—such as retrieval and verification techniques cite57†Bekoulis et al. (2021) , explainable FC cite58†Kotonya and Toni (2020) , multimodal FC cite49†Akhtar et al. (2023) , scientific FC cite59†Vladika and Matthes (2023) , justification generation cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) , and LLM-assisted FC cite61†Vykopal et al. (2024) —few consider security threats.\nL86: Notably, cite51†Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) provides a detailed taxonomy of evidence manipulation attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL87: However, to our knowledge, no existing study has comprehensively reviewed the full spectrum of adversarial attacks across the AFC pipeline—including claim manipulation, evidence poisoning, and pairwise attacks—nor proposes a unified taxonomy that spans attack targets and levels of edit granularity.\nL88: This paper addresses this critical gap by systematically examining adversarial attacks directly targeting key modules (e.g., evidence retrieval and verdict prediction) in AFC systems, while excluding related but distinct tasks such as fake news detection cite62†Wang et al. (2023) . Because AFC involves claim-specific evidence identification from large corpora, we exclude adjacent tasks such as textual entailment cite63†Jin et al. (2020) , natural language inference cite64†Zhang et al.\nL89: (2020b) , and general text classification cite65†Przybyła et al. (2024) , which lack the full AFC pipeline.\nL90: Our main contributions are as follows:\nL91: \nL92:   * •\nL93: \nL94: We provide the first systematic review of adversarial attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: \nL98: We develop a novel attacker taxonomy that categorizes attack strategies and edit granularity, offering a framework for evaluating AFC robustness under adversarial conditions.\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: \nL102: We identify key challenges in building resilient, attack-aware AFC systems, and outline promising research directions to guide future advancements in this emerging area.\nL103: ## 2 Background & Preliminaries\nL104: \nL105: Automated fact-checking (AFC) aims to verify check-worthy claims using relevant information drawn from evidence resources. Using FEVER cite66†Thorne et al. (2018a) , one of the most widely studied AFC benchmarks, as an example, the final verdict for a claim is typically classified as Supported (SUP), Refuted (REF), or NotEnoughInfo (NEI). The core AFC pipeline comprises four major tasks cite45†Guo et al. (2022) :\nL106: Claim Detection. The pipeline begins with identifying claims that are worth fact-checking. This involves assessing both the claim’s verifiability (i.e., whether the claim is specific and checkable) and its potential impact (i.e., whether misinformation could cause harm or shape public opinion) cite45†Guo et al. (2022) .\nL107: Evidence Retrieval. Next, the system searches associated sources to retrieve relevant evidence that supports or refutes the identified claim. The quality and alignment of this evidence strongly influence the downstream prediction of claim veracity cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) . For example, for the claim “The Eiffel Tower is the tallest structure in France,” a robust system should retrieve authoritative sources clarifying that while it was once the tallest, other buildings like Tour First now surpass it.\nL108: Sec. cite14†5.2 discusses how adversarial evidence can mislead retrieval and verdict stages.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdversarial Attacks Against Automated Fact-Checking: A Survey (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08463v1)\nciteturn29790view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29789view1\",\"pattern\":\"contributions\"}); Total lines: 1036\nL85: Comparison with existing surveys. While most existing surveys on FC focus on system development under benign conditions—such as retrieval and verification techniques cite57†Bekoulis et al. (2021) , explainable FC cite58†Kotonya and Toni (2020) , multimodal FC cite49†Akhtar et al. (2023) , scientific FC cite59†Vladika and Matthes (2023) , justification generation cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) , and LLM-assisted FC cite61†Vykopal et al. (2024) —few consider security threats.\nL86: Notably, cite51†Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) provides a detailed taxonomy of evidence manipulation attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL87: However, to our knowledge, no existing study has comprehensively reviewed the full spectrum of adversarial attacks across the AFC pipeline—including claim manipulation, evidence poisoning, and pairwise attacks—nor proposes a unified taxonomy that spans attack targets and levels of edit granularity.\nL88: This paper addresses this critical gap by systematically examining adversarial attacks directly targeting key modules (e.g., evidence retrieval and verdict prediction) in AFC systems, while excluding related but distinct tasks such as fake news detection cite62†Wang et al. (2023) . Because AFC involves claim-specific evidence identification from large corpora, we exclude adjacent tasks such as textual entailment cite63†Jin et al. (2020) , natural language inference cite64†Zhang et al.\nL89: (2020b) , and general text classification cite65†Przybyła et al. (2024) , which lack the full AFC pipeline.\nL90: Our main contributions are as follows:\nL91: \nL92:   * •\nL93: \nL94: We provide the first systematic review of adversarial attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: \nL98: We develop a novel attacker taxonomy that categorizes attack strategies and edit granularity, offering a framework for evaluating AFC robustness under adversarial conditions.\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: \nL102: We identify key challenges in building resilient, attack-aware AFC systems, and outline promising research directions to guide future advancements in this emerging area.\nL103: ## 2 Background & Preliminaries\nL104: \nL105: Automated fact-checking (AFC) aims to verify check-worthy claims using relevant information drawn from evidence resources. Using FEVER cite66†Thorne et al. (2018a) , one of the most widely studied AFC benchmarks, as an example, the final verdict for a claim is typically classified as Supported (SUP), Refuted (REF), or NotEnoughInfo (NEI). The core AFC pipeline comprises four major tasks cite45†Guo et al. (2022) :\nL106: Claim Detection. The pipeline begins with identifying claims that are worth fact-checking. This involves assessing both the claim’s verifiability (i.e., whether the claim is specific and checkable) and its potential impact (i.e., whether misinformation could cause harm or shape public opinion) cite45†Guo et al. (2022) .\nL107: Evidence Retrieval. Next, the system searches associated sources to retrieve relevant evidence that supports or refutes the identified claim. The quality and alignment of this evidence strongly influence the downstream prediction of claim veracity cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) . For example, for the claim “The Eiffel Tower is the tallest structure in France,” a robust system should retrieve authoritative sources clarifying that while it was once the tallest, other buildings like Tour First now surpass it.\nL108: Sec. cite14†5.2 discusses how adversarial evidence can mislead retrieval and verdict stages.\nL109: Verdict Prediction. This stage classifies the claim based on retrieved evidence. Most systems use a binary (True/False) or ternary (SUP/REF/NEI) classification scheme cite66†Thorne et al. (2018a) , though some adopt more fine-grained verdict labels reflecting degrees of truthfulness cite67†Alhindi et al. (2018) ; cite68†Augenstein et al. (2019) .\nL110: Adversarial attacks may target this step either by introducing misleading claims (see Sec. cite11†5.1 ) or by distorting evidence (see Sec. cite14†5.2 ) to trigger misclassification.\nL111: Justification Production. Finally, some AFC systems provide textual justification for their verdicts. These explanations clarify how evidence supports or refutes the claim and may incorporate reasoning, common sense inference, or attribution of assumptions cite45†Guo et al. (2022) ; cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) . Justifications enhance not only transparency but also trustworthiness and persuasive power—particularly important in adversarial contexts cite69†Zeng and Gao (2024) ; cite70†He et al. (2025) .\nL112: Figure cite48†1 illustrates the core AFC pipeline and highlights how adversarial manipulations of claims, evidence, or claim-evidence pairs can compromise each stage.\nL1001: Test FC systems: KGAT cite247†Liu et al. (2020) ; BERT cite92†Devlin et al. (2019) , CorefBERT, CoreRoBERTa cite106†Ye et al. (2020) ; MLA cite107†Kruengkrai et al. (2021) ; RoBERTAa cite98†Liu et al. (2019) ;\nL1002: ALBERT cite99†Lan et al. (2020) ; BM25 cite253†Robertson et al. (1994) ; QR-BERT cite109†Samarinas et al. (2020) .\nL1003: \nL1004:   * 1\nL1005: \nL1006: An ensemble of three FC models, i.e., BERT, RoBERTa, and ALBERT, in cite100†Atanasova et al. (2022) .\nL1007: \nL1008:   * 2\nL1009: Statistics for Acc are reported under stance filtering for both Claim-aligned Re-writing and Supporting Generation attacks, and under retrieval filtering for the Claim-aligned\nL1010: Re-writing${}_{\\text{Ret}}$ attack.\nL1011: \nL1012:   * 3\nL1013: \nL1014: Statistics for Macro-F1 score on claim verification results before and after attack.\nL1015: \nL1016:   * 4\nL1017: \nL1018: Statistics for Recall@5 on gold evidence retrieval with adversarial evidence included.\nL1019: \nL1020: Table 7: Evaluation of adversarial evidence attacks’ performance against test FC systems on FEVER 1.0.\nL1021: Experimental support, please cite254†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite255†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL1022: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL1023: \nL1024: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL1025: \nL1026:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL1027: \nL1028: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL1029: Our team has already identified cite256†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL1030: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite257†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite258†developer contributions†github.com .\nL1031: \nL1032: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite259†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL1033: cite260†About†info.arxiv.org · cite261†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite262†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite263†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite264†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite265†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite266†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite267†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L1034: \nL1035: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdversarial Attacks Against Automated Fact-Checking: A Survey (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08463v1)\nciteturn29790view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29789view1\",\"pattern\":\"taxonomy\"}); Total lines: 1036\nL74:   1. 1.\nL75: \nL76: What are the state-of-the-art adversarial techniques targeting the AFC pipeline? (Sec. cite9†4 )\nL77: \nL78:   2. 2.\nL79: \nL80: How well have current defenses developed in response to existing attacks? (Sec. cite19†6 )\nL81: \nL82:   3. 3.\nL83: \nL84: What are future directions for resilient, attack-aware AFC systems? (Sec. cite23†7 )\nL85: Comparison with existing surveys. While most existing surveys on FC focus on system development under benign conditions—such as retrieval and verification techniques cite57†Bekoulis et al. (2021) , explainable FC cite58†Kotonya and Toni (2020) , multimodal FC cite49†Akhtar et al. (2023) , scientific FC cite59†Vladika and Matthes (2023) , justification generation cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) , and LLM-assisted FC cite61†Vykopal et al. (2024) —few consider security threats.\nL86: Notably, cite51†Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) provides a detailed taxonomy of evidence manipulation attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL87: However, to our knowledge, no existing study has comprehensively reviewed the full spectrum of adversarial attacks across the AFC pipeline—including claim manipulation, evidence poisoning, and pairwise attacks—nor proposes a unified taxonomy that spans attack targets and levels of edit granularity.\nL88: This paper addresses this critical gap by systematically examining adversarial attacks directly targeting key modules (e.g., evidence retrieval and verdict prediction) in AFC systems, while excluding related but distinct tasks such as fake news detection cite62†Wang et al. (2023) . Because AFC involves claim-specific evidence identification from large corpora, we exclude adjacent tasks such as textual entailment cite63†Jin et al. (2020) , natural language inference cite64†Zhang et al.\nL89: (2020b) , and general text classification cite65†Przybyła et al. (2024) , which lack the full AFC pipeline.\nL90: Our main contributions are as follows:\nL91: \nL92:   * •\nL93: \nL94: We provide the first systematic review of adversarial attacks targeting AFC systems.\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: \nL98: We develop a novel attacker taxonomy that categorizes attack strategies and edit granularity, offering a framework for evaluating AFC robustness under adversarial conditions.\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: \nL102: We identify key challenges in building resilient, attack-aware AFC systems, and outline promising research directions to guide future advancements in this emerging area.\nL103: ## 2 Background & Preliminaries\nL104: \nL105: Automated fact-checking (AFC) aims to verify check-worthy claims using relevant information drawn from evidence resources. Using FEVER cite66†Thorne et al. (2018a) , one of the most widely studied AFC benchmarks, as an example, the final verdict for a claim is typically classified as Supported (SUP), Refuted (REF), or NotEnoughInfo (NEI). The core AFC pipeline comprises four major tasks cite45†Guo et al. (2022) :\nL106: Claim Detection. The pipeline begins with identifying claims that are worth fact-checking. This involves assessing both the claim’s verifiability (i.e., whether the claim is specific and checkable) and its potential impact (i.e., whether misinformation could cause harm or shape public opinion) cite45†Guo et al. (2022) .\nL107: Evidence Retrieval. Next, the system searches associated sources to retrieve relevant evidence that supports or refutes the identified claim. The quality and alignment of this evidence strongly influence the downstream prediction of claim veracity cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) . For example, for the claim “The Eiffel Tower is the tallest structure in France,” a robust system should retrieve authoritative sources clarifying that while it was once the tallest, other buildings like Tour First now surpass it.\nL111: Justification Production. Finally, some AFC systems provide textual justification for their verdicts. These explanations clarify how evidence supports or refutes the claim and may incorporate reasoning, common sense inference, or attribution of assumptions cite45†Guo et al. (2022) ; cite60†Eldifrawi et al. (2024) . Justifications enhance not only transparency but also trustworthiness and persuasive power—particularly important in adversarial contexts cite69†Zeng and Gao (2024) ; cite70†He et al. (2025) .\nL112: Figure cite48†1 illustrates the core AFC pipeline and highlights how adversarial manipulations of claims, evidence, or claim-evidence pairs can compromise each stage.\nL114: This section provides a structured overview of off-the-shelf adversarial attacks targeting AFC systems. We categorize attacks into three main types based on which component of the AFC pipeline they target: Adversarial claim attacks, Adversarial evidence attacks, and Adversarial claim-evidence pair attacks (discussed in Sec. cite6†1 ). Each category is further broken down based on the information source available to the attacker (e.g., original claims, evidence repositories, or open corpora).\nL115: Full details of these methods and settings are provided in Tables cite71†3 –cite72†5 in Appendix cite38†E .\nL116: Adversarial Claim Attacks. As shown in Fig. cite73†3 in Appendix cite37†D , adversarial claim attacks aim to fool the verification model by supplying manipulated or newly generated claims, mostly assuming black-box access to the verdict prediction module.\nL117: Based on the source of information used, such attacks fall into two types: (1) Evidence-guided: Generating claims by editing or recomposing sentences from existing evidence documents; and (2) Claim-guided: Manipulating original claims (e.g., via paraphrasing or adversarial transformation) to induce misclassification. Adversarial claims are typically crafted using either rule-based transformations or generative language models (LMs).\nL118: Adversarial Evidence Attacks. Figure cite74†4 in Appendix cite37†D presents the structure of adversarial evidence attacks, which aim to inject misleading or distracting evidence into the retrieval corpus, causing downstream retrieval or prediction errors.\nL119: With black-box access to the verification model, attackers can exploit four types of guidance: (1) Evidence-guided: Editing or modifying gold evidence; (2) Claim-guided: Generating misleading evidence directly from the target claim; (3) Open corpus-guided: Retrieving unrelated but plausible-looking evidence from an external corpus; (4) Retrieval-guided: Manipulating the evidence after it is retrieved for a target claim; and (5) Justification-guided: Crafting malicious evidence by leveraging the justification associated with a target claim.\nL120: The attacks use either rule-based editing or LM-based generation.\nL121: Adversarial Claim-Evidence Pair Attacks. Unlike the aforementioned categories that target individual pipeline components, claim-evidence pair attacks generate synthetic pairs that resemble valid claim-evidence relationships from the original dataset but encode contradictory or misleading content. Pairs are generated using either rule-based techniques or large language models (LLMs), as illustrated in Figure cite75†5 in Appendix cite37†D .\nL122: ## 4 Taxonomy of Adversarial Attacks\nL123: To make sense of the diverse and rapidly growing space of adversarial attacks against AFC systems, we propose a unified, technique-centric taxonomy. While previous surveys have primarily focused on the design of FC systems including dataset construction, model architectures, and pipeline components, relatively little attention has been paid to adversarial methods that actively challenge these systems.\nL124: Our goal is to synthesize existing attack techniques through the lens of their operational behavior, offering a structured understanding of how and where they compromise the AFC pipeline.\nL125: Specifically, we organize attacks based on two dimensions: (1) attack target — identifying the specific component of the AFC pipeline being compromised (i.e., verdict prediction or evidence retrieval), which aligns closely with system-level vulnerabilities; and (2) edit granularity — specifying the structural level at which adversarial perturbations are applied, ranging from subtle character-level edits to full sentence or article modifications, which influences both detectability and generalization performance.\nL126: This taxonomy allows us to systematically compare attack strategies across diverse components of the AFC pipeline and to highlight trends in their implementation, effectiveness, and robustness implications. Fig. cite76†2 provides an overview of this taxonomy, which we elaborate on in the following subsections. Corresponding empirical results are discussed in Sections cite11†5.1 –cite17†5.3 .\nL127: cite77†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: A technical taxonomy of adversarial attacks against AFC.\nL128: Besides the taxonomy, we provide structured summaries of each attack type in Appendix cite38†E , reporting key characteristics including attack target, method, and target dataset, allowing a deeper comparative analysis across techniques. Other factors such as model access (black-box vs. white-box), automation strategy (rule-based or LM-based), and semantic preservation, are reported less consistently across studies and are less suitable as primary organizational axes.\nL129: We include these dimensions in our comparison (Tables cite71†3 –cite72†5 in Appendix cite38†E ), which provides a more fine-grained analysis across attacks. We consider these dimensions complementary to our taxonomy, and promising candidates for future extensions or alternative schemes.\nL130: Next, we delve into the three categories outlined in the taxonomy—Adversarial Claim Attacks, Adversarial Evidence Attacks, and Adversarial Claim-Evidence Pair Attacks—and examine their mechanisms across attack target and edit granularity.\nL131: ## 5 Methodology of Adversarial Attacks\nL132: ### 5.1 Adversarial Claim Attacks\nL133: Adversarial Claim Attacks target the claim component of fact-checking pipelines by either generating new claims or manipulating existing ones to mislead FC models. Among the 38 surveyed attacks, 16 focus on sentence-level generation, while 22 involve manipulations at the character or wordlevel, as shown in Fig. cite76†2 . Most studies evaluate attacks using the FEVER 1.0/2.0 benchmark cite66†Thorne et al. (2018a) ; cite78†Thorne et al.\nL134: (2019b) , but a universal evaluation framework—with standardized models, metrics, and perturbation budgets—is still lacking.\nL135: #### 5.1.1 Generation-Based Attacks\nL136: Rule-Based and Game-Inspired Generation. Rule-based strategies, including Model-targeting (with Semantically Equivalent Adversarial Rules (SEARs)) and Dataset Bias, generate adversarial claims that preserve semantic meaning while inducing misclassifications in FC models cite53†Thorne et al. (2019a) ; cite79†Ribeiro et al. (2018) . These approaches reveal several key insights.\nL137: First, the Dataset Bias attack outperforms other rule-based methods by producing grammatically coherent, label-consistent claims that significantly degrade model performance—particularly in systems like Papelo cite80†Malon (2018) and Enhanced ESIM cite81†Hanselowski et al. (2018) .\nL138: Second, attacks such as Controversy, NotClear, SubsetNum, and Multi-hop introduce ambiguity, underspecification, or the need for multi-hop reasoning, thereby challenging a model’s ability to generalize beyond surface-level heuristics cite82†Kim and Allan (2019) ; cite83†Hidey et al. (2020) . Third, the Game-play attack enhances diversity and realism by involving human annotators in competitive settings to craft difficult yet plausible claims, further broadening the adversarial landscape cite84†Eisenschlos et al.\nL139: (2021) .\nL140: Language Model (LM)-Assisted Generation and Colloquial Claims. Language models such as GPT-2 cite85†Radford et al. (2019) have been leveraged to generate adversarial claims through various techniques. In the Lexically-informed attack, claims are paraphrased using lexical modifications to subtly alter input semantics while preserving fluency cite53†Thorne et al. (2019a) . The Adv.\nL141: Trigger attack embeds specific triggers into input text to flip model predictions with minimal edits, maintaining both grammaticality and semantic coherence—particularly effective when shifting verdicts from “Supported” to “Refuted” cite52†Atanasova et al. (2020) . The Fact Mixing attack employs GPT-2 to generate plausible but deceptive claims by blending facts from multiple sources, thereby misleading AFC systems without compromising fluency or label consistency cite86†Niewinski et al. (2019) .\nL142: In a related vein, the Colloquial attack rephrases claims into informal, conversational language using a BART model fine-tuned on open-domain dialogue datasets cite87†Lewis et al. (2020) . This stylistic transformation significantly degrades document retrieval performance; for example, the WikiAPI retriever’s recall drops from 90% on original FEVER claims to 72.2% on their colloquial counterparts cite88†Kim et al. (2021) .\nL143: #### 5.1.2 Manipulation-Based Attacks\nL144: Beyond generation, many attacks manipulate existing claims at different levels of granularity. At the character level, subtle perturbations such as character swapping, deletions, insertions, homoglyph substitutions (from the Unicode security dictionary), and LEET-style transformations can corrupt token representations and mislead AFC models cite54†Mamta and Cocarascu (2025) . At the word level, manipulations alter semantics while maintaining surface plausibility.\nL260:   * Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) Sahar Abdelnabi and Mario Fritz. 2023. cite127†Fact-Saboteurs: A taxonomy of evidence manipulation attacks against Fact-Verification systems†www.usenix.org . In 32nd USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 23), pages 6719–6736.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdversarial Attacks Against Automated Fact-Checking: A Survey (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.08463v1)\nciteturn29790view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn29789view1\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 1036\nL244: Vulnerability testing of LLM-based systems. Although recent work cite54†Mamta and Cocarascu (2025) has explored the robustness of representative LLMs for FC against handcrafted perturbations on claims, future research is expected to encompass not only adversarial claims generated by other attack techniques but also adversarial evidence and claim-evidence pairs from the other two categories.\nL245: Additionally, being aware of potential misuse of LLMs, it is critial for future work to evaluate how LLM-generated content (e.g., through specially designed prompts) affects the robustness of AFC systems. For instance, cite125†Sakib et al. (2025) explores how subtle prompt manipulations can induce factual inconsistencies in LLM outputs, while cite126†Zou et al.\nL246: (2025) investigates that only a few malicious texts injected into the knowledge database of a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system could result in significant performance degradation across general NLP tasks. These attacks expose vulnerabilities in LLM reasoning, factual grounding, and knowledge governance, highlighting the need for robust fact-checking tailored to generative models.\nL247: ## 8 Conclusion\nL248: \nL249: This work positions a technical view of adversarial attacks against AFC systems, outlining the pipeline for crafting target-built adversarial instances (claim, evidence, or claim-evidence pairs) to interact with these systems. We introduce a new taxonomy of attack techniques, discuss their destructive effects, and examine existing defense strategies. Finally, key challenges and opportunities are highlighted to guide future research in this emerging area.\nL250: ## Acknowledgments\nL251: \nL252: This work was supported by the Australian Research Council Project DP230100899, Macquarie University Data Horizons Research Centre and Applied AI Research Centre.\nL253: ## Limitations\nL254: While we focus on works that directly target system-level vulnerabilities in automated fact-checking (AFC) through adversarial attacks, we exclude broader research on input manipulation or text generation for other tasks such as textual entailment, natural language inference, and general text classification.\nL255: Given the importance of building resilient, attack-aware AFC systems to counter real-world misinformation, our goal is to address this critical gap by providing a systematic investigation of adversarial attacks specifically targeting AFC systems.\nL256: ## Ethics Statement\nL257: This research undertakes an investigation into adversarial attacks against automated fact-checking (AFC) systems with the aim of systematically assessing their vulnerabilities and informing the development of more robust verification tools. Recognizing that our findings could inadvertently reveal methods for spreading false information, our central ethical commitment is to defense: to support the creation of attack-aware and resilient AFC systems.\nL258: The broader ethical significance of this work lies in its potential to strengthen the integrity of information ecosystems, which is essential for combating misinformation and fostering informed public discourse. We call upon future researchers to build upon our work with rigorous attention to ethical deployment, fairness across demographic groups, and transparency in system design.\nL259: ## References\nL260:   * Abdelnabi and Fritz (2023) Sahar Abdelnabi and Mario Fritz. 2023. cite127†Fact-Saboteurs: A taxonomy of evidence manipulation attacks against Fact-Verification systems†www.usenix.org . In 32nd USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 23), pages 6719–6736.\nL261:   * Akhtar et al. (2023) Mubashara Akhtar, Michael Schlichtkrull, Zhijiang Guo, Oana Cocarascu, Elena Simperl, and Andreas Vlachos. 2023. cite128†Multimodal automated fact-checking: A survey†aclanthology.org . In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023, pages 5430–5448.\nL262:   * Alhindi et al. (2018) Tariq Alhindi, Savvas Petridis, and Smaranda Muresan. 2018. cite129†Where is your evidence: Improving fact-checking by justification modeling†doi.org . In Proceedings of the First Workshop on Fact Extraction and VERification (FEVER), pages 85–90.\nL263:   * Aly and Vlachos (2022) Rami Aly and Andreas Vlachos. 2022. cite130†Natural logic-guided autoregressive multi-hop document retrieval for fact verification†doi.org . In Proceedings of the 2022 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 6123–6135.\nL264:   * Alzantot et al. (2018) Moustafa Alzantot, Yash Sharma, Ahmed Elgohary, Bo-Jhang Ho, Mani Srivastava, and Kai-Wei Chang. 2018. cite131†Generating natural language adversarial examples†doi.org . In Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 2890–2896.\nL265:   * Atanasova et al. (2022) Pepa Atanasova, Jakob Grue Simonsen, Christina Lioma, and Isabelle Augenstein. 2022. cite132†Fact checking with insufficient evidence†doi.org . Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics, 10:746–763.\nL266:   * At